C[54] = dict(
    notes=['Standards and methods change, but principles last longer. PMBOK’s methods are built on them. So when no method covers your situation, you can reason from a principle: “What would good stewardship look like here?”',
           '**Prescriptive** means “you must do exactly this”. Building codes are prescriptive: the regulation says the required beam size. Project management can’t work that way, because every project differs. A rule for a mobile app could be wrong for a hospital system.',
           'Principles **guide behavior**, so two PMs can follow the same principle differently and both be right. One team might involve stakeholders through weekly demos, another through monthly reviews.',
           'Principles and morals are different things. “Tailor to the context” is good advice, but not a moral issue. Stewardship does touch on morals. That’s why the slide says “can, but do not necessarily” reflect morals.'],
    tips=['**Principle = compass, not GPS.** It points you in the right direction but doesn’t give you turn-by-turn directions.',
          'Trap words: **“must”, “always”, “exactly”** in a statement about principles usually make it false.'],
    exam=[truth('The principles of project management are prescriptive rules that must be followed exactly.', False,
                'They are not prescriptive; they guide behavior and can be applied in many ways.',
                'In some professions principles are rules, but the slide says project management’s aren’t. They guide behavior, and there are many ways to stay aligned with them.'),
          truth('Project management principles always reflect morals.', False,
                'The slide says they “can, but do not necessarily” reflect morals.',
                'Watch out for “always”. A principle like tailoring is practical advice, with nothing moral about it.'),
          pick('What do principles for a profession serve as?',
               'Foundational guidelines for strategy, decision making, and problem solving', 'This is the slide’s first point. Standards and methods often build on them.',
               ('Step-by-step procedures for each task', 'That describes a method or a process, not a principle.'),
               ('Legal requirements in every profession', 'Some professions treat them as laws, but it isn’t universal, and project management doesn’t.'),
               ('Performance targets for staff', 'Principles guide behavior. They don’t set targets.')),
          pick('Two PMs both apply “engage stakeholders” differently: weekly demos and monthly reviews. What does this show?',
               'Principles are broad, so there are many ways to stay aligned with them', 'The slide says they’re broadly based, giving room for different approaches.',
               ('One PM is wrong, since principles allow only one way', 'Principles don’t prescribe one way.'),
               ('The principle has been violated by both', 'Both are engaging stakeholders, so both are aligned.'),
               ('Principles apply only in large organizations', 'Nothing limits them this way.'))])
